<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  PLAN 53 P3 / X16 (QBX-181). MODE A OVER AN EVENT COLUMN: a pass over the
  MIDDLE of a long MIDI part folds the part and the pass into ONE union
  column, and the part's own notes are still there.

  The verb is driven directly, so the whole case is arithmetic: no capture
  device, no transport, no wall-clock jitter anywhere in it.

  WHY X16 IS THE CELL. The ticket's own words: "an event clip [0, 32 bars), a
  pass over bars 8-16 -> one column". That is R2 (s < a and e > b) with Kp
  (the touched child is a plain clip), the one cell where the pass lies
  strictly inside the existing material, and the one the EVENT path could not
  express at all before P3:

    * `eventColumns` returned `cols.front()` and the planner used that ONE
      column, so a pass spanning several event clips landed on the earliest
      and left the rest alone - the performance over their bars was simply not
      there;
    * there was NO PREDATES RULE. A column starting BEFORE the pass cannot
      receive it as a take (a take is an alternative for the same window, so
      it would need material from before the recording began), and nothing
      said so: the pass became a take at the COLUMN's origin, i.e. EARLIER
      THAN IT WAS PLAYED. Here that is 8 bars early. The audio side fixed this
      in B5 (`place_recording_over_earlier_clip`); the event side never had it.

  Mode A answers both at once, with the SAME planner the audio side uses
  (`objects/recplan`, QBX-188): the part and the pass become takes of one
  column, the pass active over its own bars, the part answering everywhere
  else.

  THE NUMBERS ARE CLOSED FORMS. PPQ is 960 and the project is 4/4 at 120 BPM
  on 48 kHz, so one tick is 25 frames and one bar is 3840 ticks = 96000
  frames:

    the part    bars 0-32   = 122880 ticks = [0, 3072000)
    the pass    bars 8-16   =  30720 ticks = [768000, 1536000)

  so C = [0, 3072000), takes O [0, 3072000) and R [768000, 1536000), and the
  map is 0 -> O, 768000 -> R, 1536000 -> O. That is §3.3's "R2 · Kp" row
  verbatim, which is the point: ONE matrix, two content kinds.

  WHAT IS NOT GATED HERE, and is filed rather than hidden. The FEED holds the
  PASS's notes only: `STakeStack::resolveEventClip` resolves through the
  ACTIVE take, so wherever that take has no material the column plays SILENCE
  instead of the take the comp map names. P3 extent-shifts that resolve - which
  is why the pass sounds at 768000 below and not at 0, where it sounded before
  - and the comp half needs what the event path has no equivalent of, a
  `twCompColumn` (plan 53 §4.1 said so). FILED AS QBX-189, with the fix
  sketched; it is asserted below as what it IS, so the day it changes this case
  says so.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="midi_rec_union_r2">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>

    <!-- The part: 32 bars, a note at bar 0 and a note at bar 8. The second
         one sits exactly where the pass begins, which is deliberate - it is
         the note a planner that placed the pass at the column's origin would
         have landed on top of. -->
    <insert-midi-clip trackPath="0" timePos="0" duration="122880" name="Part"/>
    <add-note clip="0,0" tick="0"     dur="480" key="60" velocity="100" channel="0"/>
    <add-note clip="0,0" tick="30720" dur="480" key="62" velocity="100" channel="0"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="3072000"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="none"/>

    <place-midi-recording trackPath="0" timePos="768000" durationTicks="30720"
                          mode="takes" name="R">
      <n tick="0"   dur="480" key="72" velocity="100" channel="0"/>
      <n tick="960" dur="480" key="74" velocity="100" channel="0"/>
    </place-midi-recording>

    <!-- ONE column, at the part's own child index, spanning the part. Under
         the old planner this was a column too - but one whose new take covered
         the WHOLE part, so the part was inaudible everywhere and `extents`
         read "0-3072000;0-3072000". -->
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="1"
                        extents="0-3072000;768000-1536000"
                        comp="0:0;768000:1;1536000:0" duration="3072000"/>
    <!-- ...and no second child appeared beside it. -->
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- X16's own assertion: THE PART'S NOTES ARE STILL THERE, on take 0, at
         their own ticks. `scope="column"` does not exist and is not added -
         `scope="clip" take=` is the question, because a take IS a clip. -->
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" take="0" kind="noteon" count="2"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" take="0" contains="key=60"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" take="0" contains="key=62"/>
    <!-- ...and the pass is take 1, carrying its own two notes at ITS ticks
         (tick 0 of the pass, not tick 30720 of the part). -->
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" take="1" kind="noteon" count="2"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" take="1" contains="key=72"/>

    <!-- WHAT THE INSTRUMENT HEARS, which no structural assertion can give.
         The pass sounds AT BAR 8 - 768000, and 792000 for its second note, one
         quarter later. Before P3's extent shift it sounded at 0 and 24000: the
         take was asked for its material in the COLUMN's frames and answered in
         its own, so a recording over bars 8-16 played at bar 0.

         The part's notes are NOT in this list, and that is the filed
         limitation above, not this case's claim. -->
    <assert-midi-events scope="feed" trackPath="0" kind="noteon" count="2"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="feed" trackPath="0" kind="noteon" at="768000"
                        key="72"
                        count="1"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="feed" trackPath="0" kind="noteon" at="792000"
                        key="74"
                        count="1"/>

    <!-- ONE UNDO for the whole pass: the part is a plain clip again, at its
         own length, with its own two notes and nothing else. A merge that
         could not be undone would leave the user's part inside a column.

         THIS IS WHERE THE PASS USED TO SEGFAULT (QBX-181). `merge-columns`'
         inverse reads its operand recipe back out of the merge ACTION, so a
         stack-local composite — which is what this verb had, correctly, while
         every child it emitted built a self-contained inverse — left
         `unmerge-columns` pointing at a destroyed action. The plan is held
         now, exactly as `place-recording` holds its own.

         The log line "`unmerge-columns` produced no inverse, so the WHOLE step
         is not undoable" fires here and is FALSE; it is pre-existing and filed
         as QBX-187. The step is undoable — which is what these four
         assertions say, and what the redo below says from the other side. -->
    <undo/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="none"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="3072000"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" kind="noteon" count="2"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- ...AND REDO PUTS IT BACK, identically. That is the other half of
         QBX-187's claim: the step is undoable through
         `SActionUndoCommand::redo()` re-applying the forward, and a re-applied
         forward RE-PLANS against the lane the undo left — so this also gates
         that the undo left the lane in a state the plan can be made on again.
         The redo also restores the undo/redo balance that `verify-undo` below
         checks. -->
    <redo/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="1"
                        extents="0-3072000;768000-1536000"
                        comp="0:0;768000:1;1536000:0" duration="3072000"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" take="1" contains="key=72"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

  <verify-undo/>

</SActionScript>
